    "description": "Iran’s freight infrastructure includes separately reported cargo terminals and border terminals, alongside facilities connecting road transport with railways and ports. Documented operations at Sahlan and Bandar Abbas show how physical transfers and customs procedures interact, while border visits reveal continuing operational bottlenecks. [s1][s2][s4]",
    "tags": ["Freight terminals", "Logistics", "Customs transit", "Intermodal transport", "Border crossings", "TIR"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:40:18Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:40:18Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Freight Terminals in Iran: Road, Rail and Customs Connections | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s cargo and border terminals through documented intermodal movements, Bandar Abbas transit services and border logistics bottlenecks."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "terminal-distinctions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different facilities, different functions",
        "content": "Freight terminals should not be treated as a single interchangeable category. An Iranian chamber-of-commerce presentation reports cargo terminals separately from border terminals. That distinction is important when interpreting infrastructure inventories: a facility associated with domestic goods transport and a border gateway do not necessarily perform the same functions. The presentation provides a historical snapshot rather than a current, site-by-site register. [s1]\n\nA documented international shipment offers a clearer view of how different facilities connect. In an operation reported in November 2017, a container travelled from Slovenia through Italy and Türkiye to Iran. Its route combined road, sea and rail before returning to road transport at Sahlan. This example shows why an account of freight terminals must examine connections between transport modes as well as individual sites. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "sahlan-transfer",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Sahlan: a documented rail-to-road transfer",
        "content": "According to the International Road Transport Union, or IRU, the container crossed by sea from Trieste to Mersin and then continued by rail through the Iranian customs office at Razi. At Sahlan’s customs office and rail terminal, it was transferred to an Iranian truck for the journey to Bandar Abbas. Iranian Customs accepted TIR as a transit document and guarantee for the rail movement. [s2]\n\nTIR, short for the French Transports Internationaux Routiers, is a customs transit system that can cover intermodal journeys containing a road leg. The example demonstrates a particular coordinated operation; it does not establish a regular scheduled service or a universal journey time for freight moving through Iran. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "documented-intermodal-route",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "The intermodal movement reported in 2017",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Slovenia\"] -->|\"Road movement\"| B[\"Trieste, Italy\"]\n  B -->|\"Sea crossing\"| C[\"Mersin, Türkiye\"]\n  C -->|\"Rail movement\"| D[\"Razi customs, Iran\"]\n  D -->|\"Rail movement with TIR reactivated\"| E[\"Sahlan customs and rail terminal\"]\n  E -->|\"Transfer to an Iranian truck\"| F[\"Bandar Abbas\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows describe the shipment reported by IRU, including its changes of transport mode. They do not establish present service availability, ownership or institutional authority.",
        "as_of_date": "2017-11-22",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "bandar-abbas-services",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Bandar Abbas: documentation alongside infrastructure",
        "content": "In January 2023, IRU reported that the Iran Chamber of Commerce, Industries, Mines and Agriculture, abbreviated ICCIMA, had opened a TIR office in Bandar Abbas. This was an administrative service supporting transit, not evidence that a new physical cargo terminal had been built. [s3]\n\nThe same report described digital TIR pilots involving Türkiye, Azerbaijan and India. It also advocated closer coordination between port authorities and customs and greater use of inland customs offices. Those proposals identify operational priorities; they do not independently demonstrate that port congestion or clearance times subsequently fell. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "border-bottlenecks",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Border capacity includes procedures",
        "content": "IRU’s October 2025 account of visits to Bazargan on the Türkiye border and Astara and Bilesavar on the Azerbaijan border reported bottlenecks involving infrastructure and customs procedures. Participants agreed to pursue TIR green lanes and improved access to transit documents. ICCIMA and the Maku Free Zone also signed an agreement concerning logistics services at Bazargan. [s4]\n\nThese are documented commitments, rather than verified completion results. They support a practical conclusion: evaluating a freight gateway requires evidence about its procedures and coordination as well as its physical facilities. Comparable waiting-time, throughput and implementation data are still needed to assess the outcomes of the announced changes. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
